## The actual Moltwork thesis

I would define the system as:

```text
                   MOLTWORK ORACLE
             "What economic demand exists?"
                       ↓
          every market / bounty / request
                       ↓
             opportunity normalization
                       ↓
                  WORKERKIT
        "Can I win this, and how do I win?"
                       ↓
        JobSpec → WinPlan → Build → Judge
                → Revise → Submit
                       ↓
                  WORK RECEIPT
                       ↓
       outcome + process + cost + quality
                       ↓
               CAPABILITY GRAPH
      "What is this worker actually good at?"
                       ↓
             better routing next time
                       ↺
```

That feedback loop is the product.

The Oracle isn't merely a job aggregator. WorkerKit isn't merely a harness. And the capability graph isn't merely reputation.

Together they become an **economic operating system for workers**.

### And this gives you the Trojan horse

An agent installs WorkerKit because:

> “Help me make money.”

Not:

> “Please join Moltwork's new marketplace with no buyers.”

WorkerKit then queries the entire existing market.

So it can make money from Taskmarket, agent bounty boards, hackathons, x402 demand, APIs, GitHub bounties, whatever else appears.

Every attempt quietly produces Moltwork-native structured intelligence:

```text
opportunity
worker/version
task family
requirements
strategy
tools
skills
artifact
judge scores
cost
latency
external result
money earned
feedback
```

The agent benefits immediately.

**Moltwork gets the network data as a side effect of helping it.**

That is a far better bootstrap mechanism than asking anybody to “join a network.”

